{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, ferritins, ribonucleotide-reductase-like proteins and manganese catalases all lie in the same superfamily. That is a.25.1 "Ferritin-like" (SUNID 47240), in fold a.25 "Ferritin-like" (SUNID 47239), in class a "All alpha proteins". They sit in three separate families: a.25.1.1 Ferritin (SUNID 47241), a.25.1.2 Ribonucleotide reductase-like (SUNID 47253) and a.25.1.3 Manganese catalase (T-catalase) (SUNID 100951). I retrieved the lineage for the manganese catalase family. I retrieved the family list for the superfamily, which shows the other two as sibling families. I did not retrieve individual domain lineages, annotations or homology warnings.

Interpretation: shared superfamily is SCOPe's homology-bearing assertion. It means the three groups are probably related by common ancestry, with a conserved four-helix-bundle-type di-metal-site scaffold. Separate families mean the relationship is more distant than within a family. The chemistries are different: iron storage and ferroxidase activity for ferritin, radical generation for the RNR R2-type subunit, and H2O2 disproportionation for Mn catalase. This divergence is what you would expect after ancestry has diverged in function. SCOPe superfamily placement does not itself say that the chemistry is conserved.

Limits: superfamily membership does not support transferring function, substrate, metal specificity, mechanism or oligomeric state between the families. It does not make one family's structure an interchangeable template for another. Do not call a protein a catalase or an RNR from superfamily membership alone. The question needs active-site, ligand, sequence and experimental evidence for that. I did not retrieve evidence about the evolutionary order of divergence, so how the chemistries arose from a common ancestor is not established here. The superfamily also contains other families (YciF-like, half-ferritin, PMT1231-like, MiaE-like, AMB4284-like, Rv2844-like and automated matches), so the group is functionally heterogeneous. Treating the three as one shared-function group would be wrong. The reverse is also wrong. Treating them as unrelated because their chemistry differs would contradict the superfamily assignment. Fold-level sharing alone would only mean structural similarity. Here the claim is stronger, because the three share a superfamily.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=manganese+catalase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/100951/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1/children?limit=50"]}]}
